Another IPTV Player — a11 fork
================================

A free, open-source IPTV player for Xtream Codes and M3U/M3U8 playlists.
This fork extends the Flutter Windows application and adds a companion LG
webOS client for live TV, movies, and series. It has no analytics, telemetry,
ads, or bundled IPTV service.

This project is not an IPTV provider. It hosts, sells, and distributes no
content. Bring your own compatible service and use only content you are
authorized to access.


Fork highlights
---------------

* Windows desktop — the Flutter app manages playlists, provider credentials,
  catalogues, favorites, history, category preferences, channel shortcuts,
  EPG data, subtitles, and playback.
* LG webOS client — a TV-focused interface for browsing and playing the
  desktop library without storing provider credentials on the television.
* Secure LAN pairing — short-lived pairing codes and revocable device tokens;
  API responses use opaque item identities and do not expose upstream URLs.
* Lightweight synchronization — the TV receives bounded shelves and paged
  category data while the desktop remains authoritative for shared state.
* Playback compatibility — credential-safe proxying, HLS rewriting, FFmpeg
  remux, and transcode fallbacks for formats the TV cannot play directly.
* Resume and history — synchronized progress, continue watching, separate
  Resume and Play From Start actions, and desktop fallback when TV storage is
  unavailable.
* TV controls — remote-friendly navigation, programme up/down zapping, Yellow
  channel list, Blue shortcut-page cycling, and numeric channel shortcuts.
* Tracks and subtitles — audio and subtitle selection, shared language
  preferences, OpenSubtitles support, WebVTT delivery, timing offset, and
  subtitle styling.
* EPG improvements — provider-compatible schedule fallback plus tooling for
  scheduled UK and Greek XMLTV guide updates.
* Library organization — category ordering, hidden-category and radio filters,
  per-category sorting, bulk category toggles, search, favorites, and history.
* Playback fixes — more resilient webOS 4K resume, one-connection provider
  handling, larger live-stream buffering, a compatible User-Agent, and
  five-second Left/Right seeking in the desktop movie player.
* Older-TV support — the webOS build targets the legacy Chromium environment
  found on devices such as webOS 4.4 and includes bounded cache/quota recovery.


Architecture
------------

The Windows application is the source of truth. It stores credentials and the
full catalogue, exposes an authenticated LAN API, and owns favorites, history,
category settings, shortcuts, EPG processing, and compatibility media
sessions. The webOS app owns TV navigation, presentation, LG lifecycle events,
and native audio/video/subtitle integration. Desktop-owned values win when
state is synchronized.

The desktop server listens on TCP port `32145`; discovery uses UDP port
`32146`. Provider credentials, bearer tokens, pairing codes, and signed media
URLs must never be committed or included in diagnostics.


Current source versions
-----------------------

* Windows Flutter application: `1.7.6+37`
* LG webOS application: `1.7.16`
* Desktop/webOS LAN API: version `1`

Installed versions and release artifacts can differ from source. See
`PROJECT_NOTES.md` and `shared/docs/webos/current-state.md` before building,
installing, deploying, or testing on a physical TV.


Getting started
---------------

1. Download a suitable build from this fork's releases:
   https://github.com/a11otrios/another-iptv-player-a11/releases
2. Install and launch the Windows application.
3. Add Xtream Codes credentials or an M3U/M3U8 URL or file.
4. For LG TV use, build and install the webOS package, then pair it with the
   desktop application on the same local network.

The Windows app must be reachable through Windows Firewall from the TV. A
localhost connection alone does not prove LAN access. Follow the deployment
guide before replacing an installed desktop executable or launching a TV
package.


Development
-----------

The main source locations are:

* Windows desktop and LAN server: `apps/flutter`
* LG webOS client: `apps/webos`
* Shared engineering documentation: `shared/docs`
* Client/server protocol: `shared/docs/webos-client-server-protocol.md`
* Desktop/webOS feature matrix: `shared/docs/webos-feature-parity.md`

Start with `PROJECT_NOTES.md`. It contains the current versions, required
documentation order, safe build/deployment procedures, physical-TV rules, and
known runtime hazards.

Typical automated checks are:

```powershell
cd apps\flutter
flutter test --no-pub
flutter analyze --no-pub

cd ..\webos
pnpm run test
pnpm run build
```

Do not launch the webOS app or start playback on a physical TV without first
confirming that the TV is free and identifying the test content. Building an
installer, installing packages, and running playback tests have additional
safety steps in `shared/docs/webos/development-and-deployment.md`.


Known limitations
-----------------

* LG's native video surface on tested hardware ignores CSS `object-fit`, so
  the in-app aspect-ratio preference does not visibly change the picture. Use
  the TV's Picture settings.
* Windows Firewall access is executable-path-specific. A source-tree build may
  work on localhost while remaining unreachable from the TV.
* Provider EPG coverage varies, and dashboard cards do not yet show Now/Next
  data before live playback begins.
* Some hardware-dependent audio, HDR, embedded-track, and lifecycle behavior
  still requires physical-device verification.

See `shared/docs/webos/known-issues.md` for evidence, workarounds, and current
validation status.


